   But I could do the other thing.
   I put my palm flat on the steel counter, where the El was still hum‐
ming through the metal, and found the place where everything over‐
lapped, the drum and the room and the train and the glyph, and I
leaned on it, the way you lean on a door that wants to open too fast. I
slowed my own breath. I brought the Garden’s gain down one notch,
then another. The gold went from searing to warm. The edges of the
spiral eased like a held note being let go. Nobody in that room felt any‐
thing but the best five minutes of their week.
   My nose was running. I wiped it with the back of my hand, and my
hand came away red, a thin line like a pencil mark.
   Then the floor went.
   It was Jerome in 1986. The gate was half-down over the shop win‐
dow, DISCOS QUISQUEYA in peeling paint, one fluorescent tube flick‐
ering. The bins were almost empty, a few records left up front like sur‐
vivors. At the register sat Mr. Rodríguez’s father, older now, the mus‐
tache gone to salt, with a small radio on the counter giving the day’s
numbers, shootings and then more shootings, in the voice of a man
reading something he was sorry about. He wasn’t doing anything. He
sat in the dark of his own shop with both hands flat on the glass the
way you sit with something sick.
   Outside, a sound came up from the street. A kid on a plastic bucket,
with sticks cut from a broom handle, working a rhythm out on the
curb.
   The man’s head turned. His pen began to tap the counter, a beat be‐
hind the kid, then on top of him. Then he stood, walked to the front,
put both hands on the gate, and hauled it up. Light fell out onto the
sidewalk like something spilled.
   Manny hit the last pattern, a flourish with no plan to it, and the
room came back, and the glyph folded into the brick and was gone,
and everybody lost their minds.
   Mr. Rodríguez stood by the register with one hand flat against his
chest and his eyes shut. I found out later he said it was the draft.



